# SYNOPSIS

```usage
ptyd
```

# DESCRIPTION

`ptyd` serves pseudo-terminals: a master, which a terminal program reads and writes, and a slave, which the program running on the terminal reads and writes, with a line discipline between them, as Linux's has.
svcd(8) starts it from `boot/svc/ptyd.ndb`, with the boot image at `/` and procfs(4) at `/proc`, and posts it as `/srv/ptyd`; `/lib/ns/posix` mounts it after the null devices on `/dev`, so a POSIX program finds `/dev/ptmx` and `/dev/pts/`.
It is not restarted: its terminals live only in it.

The terminals' settings are POSIX's termios, with Linux's numbers, as the POSIX personality has them; the musl back end's `tcgetattr`, `tcsetattr`, `TIOCGWINSZ`, `TIOCSWINSZ`, `tcsetpgrp` and the rest read and write a terminal's `ctl` file.

: `ptmx`
  Opening it, in any mode, makes a new terminal, and the fid opened becomes its master.
  A stat of the master gives the terminal's number as its name, which is how a program finds its slave.
  Reading the master returns what the slave's program wrote, and the echo of what was typed, waiting while there is none.
  Writing it is typing at the terminal: each byte goes through the line discipline.
  More fids for the master, made by a fork's `Tjoin` or an open of the same file, keep it open until each is closed.
: `pts/`
  A directory holding `<n>` and `<n>.ctl` for each terminal whose master is open.
: `pts/<n>`
  Terminal <n>'s slave, a device of mode 0620.
  Reading it returns typed input: in canonical mode one line at most, up to and including its newline or to where a `^D` sent it; otherwise the bytes there are.
  A read waits while there is none.
  A `^D` typed on an empty line makes the next read return nothing, the end of the file; so does a read once the master is closed and every byte typed has been read.
  Writing it sends output to the master, a newline becoming a carriage return and a newline when the terminal's `oflag` has `OPOST` and `ONLCR`.
  A write takes as much as there is room for, and waits for the master to read when there is none.
  Once the master is closed, an open or a write of the slave fails with `i/o on hungup channel`.
: `pts/<n>.ctl`
  The terminal's settings, one ndb record (see ndb(6)): `iflag`, `oflag`, `cflag` and `lflag`, in decimal; `cc`, the 32 control characters, in hexadecimal as `x"..."`; `rows` and `cols`; `pgrp`, the foreground process group, 0 for none; and `avail`, the bytes of input waiting to be read.
  Writing a record sets the settings it names and leaves the rest; a `cc` that is not 32 bytes and keys it does not know are ignored.
  The flag `flush` throws away the input waiting and the line being typed.
  A change of `rows` or `cols` sends `SIGWINCH` to the foreground group.

A terminal goes when its master's fids and its slave's are all closed.

A new terminal has Linux's defaults: `iflag` `ICRNL` and `IUTF8`, `oflag` `OPOST` and `ONLCR`, `cflag` 0277 (`CS8`, `CREAD` and `B38400`), and `lflag` `ISIG`, `ICANON`, `ECHO`, `ECHOE` and `ECHOK`; `^C` interrupts, `^\` quits, DEL erases, `^U` kills the line, `^D` ends the file and `^Z` suspends; 80 columns by 24 rows.

@node=discipline title="The line discipline"

Each byte typed at the master is taken through these steps, as the terminal's settings say:

- With `ICRNL`, a carriage return becomes a newline.
- With `ISIG`, the `VINTR`, `VQUIT` and `VSUSP` characters are echoed with a newline, the line being typed is thrown away, and the signal is sent to the foreground group: `SIGINT`, `SIGQUIT` or `SIGTSTP`.
- Without `ICANON`, the byte is input at once, and echoed.
- In canonical mode, `VERASE` or a backspace takes back the last character typed, a whole rune with `IUTF8` and a byte without; `VKILL` takes back the whole line; `VEOF` sends the line as it is, with no newline, or on an empty line the end of the file; any other byte is added to the line, which a newline sends.
- With `ECHO`, what is typed is echoed to the master: a control character other than newline and tab as `^` and a letter, a newline as a carriage return and a newline under `OPOST` and `ONLCR`, and an erased character as a backspace, a space and a backspace.

A line that reaches 4096 bytes is sent at the last whole rune that fits, and the rest starts the next line.

A signal is a note, written to `/proc/<pgrp>/notepg`, so it reaches every process in the group (ADR-0011): `interrupt` for `SIGINT`, as in Plan 9, and `posix: SIGQUIT`, `posix: SIGTSTP` or `posix: SIGWINCH` for the others.
A slave read waiting when ptyd sends a signal ends with `interrupted`, unless input has come meanwhile, so `^C` at a prompt is seen at once; a change of size ends none.

# EXAMPLES

The manifest that starts it, `boot/svc/ptyd.ndb`:

```ndb
service=ptyd program=/boot/bin/ptyd post=ptyd console entropy
mount=/ srv=bootfs
mount=/proc srv=proc
```

and the lines of `/lib/ns/posix` that put the terminals in a POSIX program's `/dev`:

```text
mount /srv/null /dev
mount -a /srv/ptyd /dev
```

# FILES

: `/srv/ptyd`
  ptyd's post.
: `/dev/ptmx`, `/dev/pts/`
  Where a POSIX namespace has its files.
: `/proc/<pgrp>/notepg`
  Where a signal is written.

