# DIAGNOSTICS

It prints `ptyd: serving /srv/ptyd` when it starts, or `ptyd: serving /srv/ptyd, without /proc: no signals` when its namespace has no `/proc`, in which case no signal is sent.
Without a listen channel it prints `ptyd: no listen channel` and exits with `no listen channel`; it exits `cannot serve` if serving fails.

An open of `ptmx` when 16 terminals are in use fails with `out of memory`, a write to a `ctl` file that is not a record with `bad message`, and an open of a directory for writing with `permission denied`.

# BUGS

It holds 16 terminals, and is not restarted.

Job control is partial: there is no `SIGTTIN` or `SIGTTOU` for a background group's reads and writes, and no controlling terminal is kept for a session.
Whoever can write a terminal's `ctl` can set its foreground group, with no session check, and so send `^C` and `^Z` to any group.

A signal goes to the note group of the process whose pid is the group's number; when that process has ended, its group gets none.
Closing the master sends no `SIGHUP`, and the master's reader is not told when the slaves are closed: its read waits.

`VMIN` and `VTIME` are ignored: a read in non-canonical mode returns what is there, waiting only while there is nothing.
Of the termios flags only `ICRNL`, `IUTF8`, `OPOST`, `ONLCR`, `ISIG`, `ICANON` and `ECHO` are acted on; the others, flow control and `cflag` among them, are kept and reported only.

Input typed while 4096 bytes wait to be read, and echo while 4096 bytes wait for the master, are dropped, as a terminal drops them, with no error; only the slave's own writes are held instead.
